The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one fix queued. Its own
rendered cause table contradicts that conclusion. The ticket held two problems, and one fix
did not close both.

## What the desk did with the ticket

The desk's engineering update, read on 28 October 2026, says **#4471 closed 05 oct**:

- **Parser — fixed:** `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. [Ticket #1](https://github.com/ckjackslack/support-kb-4471/issues/1) is closed as completed.
- **Hint — won't fix:** `booking-v3 form retired`. [Ticket #2](https://github.com/ckjackslack/support-kb-4471/issues/2) is closed as not planned.

The update is the desk's own account of the ticket after the export this page reads. The
export is dated 4 October 2026, so the counts below are that snapshot and not a live
queue. A closed ticket is not a claim that every report in it was resolved: the update
does not mention the five reports the desk read as a one-day move, and they stay open
questions below.

<Callout kind="warn">
Keep the shared parser and the booking-v3 hint apart. Intake's tag and a matching subject
line are not proof of a shared cause. The counts below are computed from unique report ids,
the tenant directory and the supplied cause rules.
</Callout>

## What the desk filed

The desk merges on the subject line alone, the rule in
[decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject). #4471 now holds more than one subject
line, so it holds more than that rule allows. This is what the desk's own logic makes of the
merged reports:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser, and it is a guess. The cause is not the tag:
it is the tenant's locale and the booking form that tenant runs.

## What the reports hold

<ProblemsTable {ticketLinks} />

## The two problems

**Month and day swapped in the shared parser.** The parser reads every date month first. A
tenant that writes the day first gets a flipped date, and its people see the booking on the
wrong day. These are the app, email and booking-v2 form reports.

**The booking form prints the wrong order for the tenant.** booking-v3 prints MM/DD/YYYY. A
day-first tenant reads that hint and types the month first, as it was told to, and the form
reads the date the tenant's way. Same symptom, different cause, different fix: the form and
the parser disagree about the same field. These are the form reports of the booking-v3
tenants.

## What the desk did

- The parser now reads the tenant's date order. Ticket #1 is closed as completed.
- booking-v3 is retired, so the form no longer prints MM/DD/YYYY to a tenant that writes
  the day first. Ticket #2 is closed as not planned, which is not a delivered fix.
- The merge rule in [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) still merges on
  the subject line, and a subject line cannot tell a swapped date from a shifted one. A
  decision is never edited after it is accepted, so a new record replaces it.
- The five open questions below are not in the update, and stay open.

## Lessons

- One ticket can hold more than one bug. Merge early, count once, but split by cause.
- A duplicate is one id. The export gives the same report more than once.
- A tag from intake is a guess. Read the tenant, and read the form the tenant runs.

